This paper investigates the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints, where $\beta>0$, $\mu\in\{0,2,4\}$, $c>0$, and $F(s)=s^2e^{4\pi\alpha s^2}$ with $\alpha\in(0,\frac{1}{4\pi])$. To find a normalized ground state solution for this equation, we apply a high-dimensional minimization flow and critical point theory approaches.
